<article class="env-definition" id="0AID">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AID">Definition <span data-tag="0AID">87.9.7</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be an affine formal algebraic space over $S$. We say $X$ is <em>McQuillan</em> if $X$ satisfies the equivalent conditions of Lemma <a href="/tag/0AIC" data-tag="0AIC">87.9.6</a>. Let $A$ be the weakly admissible topological ring associated to $X$. We say </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$X$ is <em>classical</em> if $X$ is McQuillan and $A$ is admissible (More on Algebra, Definition <a href="/tag/07E8" data-tag="07E8">15.37.1</a>), 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X$ is <em>weakly adic</em> if $X$ is McQuillan and $A$ is weakly adic (Definition <a href="/tag/0GXE" data-tag="0GXE">87.7.1</a>), 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X$ is <em>adic</em> if $X$ is McQuillan and $A$ is adic (More on Algebra, Definition <a href="/tag/07E8" data-tag="07E8">15.37.1</a>), 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X$ is <em>adic*</em> if $X$ is McQuillan, $A$ is adic, and $A$ has a finitely generated ideal of definition, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$X$ is <em>Noetherian</em> if $X$ is McQuillan and $A$ is both Noetherian and adic. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
